__d(function(e,t,o,a,s,n,i){s.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Science",textbook:"Science \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:8,chapter_title:"Body Movements",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are the four main functions of the human skeleton?",back:"(1) Shape and support: gives the body its upright shape and supports its weight. (2) Protection: skull protects brain; rib cage protects heart and lungs; vertebral column protects spinal cord. (3) Movement: provides attachment points for muscles; bones act as levers. (4) Blood cell production: bone marrow inside bones produces red and white blood cells.",memory_hook:"SPMB = Shape, Protect, Move, Blood. 'Skeletons Protect Moving Bodies.' Four jobs, one framework."},{id:"fc02",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a ball and socket joint? Name two places in the body where it is found.",back:"A ball and socket joint has a rounded ball-shaped end of one bone that fits into a cup-shaped socket of another bone. It allows movement in ALL directions \u2014 forward, backward, sideways, and rotation. Found at: (1) Shoulder joint (humerus ball fits into the socket of the scapula). (2) Hip joint (head of femur fits into the socket of the pelvis).",memory_hook:"BALL goes into a BOWL = moves in ALL directions. Shoulder and hip \u2014 the two big pivot points of your body. Ball-Bowl-All: ball in bowl = all directions."},{id:"fc03",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a hinge joint? Give two examples in the body.",back:"A hinge joint allows movement in ONLY ONE direction \u2014 bending (flexion) and straightening (extension) \u2014 like a door hinge that can only swing open and shut. Examples: (1) Elbow joint (allows the forearm to bend and straighten). (2) Knee joint (allows the lower leg to bend and straighten). Also: finger joints and toe joints.",memory_hook:"HINGE = opens ONE way. Door hinge opens and closes \u2014 just like the elbow and knee. Back and forth, no rotation allowed."},{id:"fc04",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a pivot joint? Where is it found and what movement does it allow?",back:"A pivot joint allows one bone to rotate around another in a twisting/rotating motion. Found in the neck between the atlas (1st cervical vertebra) and the axis (2nd cervical vertebra). This joint allows you to turn your head left and right \u2014 the 'no' shaking movement. Without this pivot joint, you could not rotate your head.",memory_hook:"PIVOT = spins around a PIN. 'Shake your head NO' = pivot joint in action. Atlas rotates around Axis \u2014 AA pivot."},{id:"fc05",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a fixed joint? Where in the body is it found?",back:"A fixed joint (also called an immovable or fibrous joint) is a joint where the bones are fused tightly together and allow NO movement at all. Found between the bones of the skull \u2014 these joints are called sutures (they look like saw-tooth seams on the skull). The fixed joints of the skull form the rigid protective case for the brain. Note: The lower jaw (mandible) is the only skull bone that can move \u2014 via a different joint (synovial joint at the temporomandibular joint).",memory_hook:"FIXED = stuck, cannot move. Skull bones are FIXED together \u2014 protecting the brain inside a rigid cage. SUTURES stitch the skull bones permanently."},{id:"fc06",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is cartilage? How is it different from bone? Where is it found?",back:"Cartilage is a smooth, flexible, tough connective tissue. Differences from bone: (1) Softer and more flexible than bone (you can bend it). (2) Has NO blood vessels \u2014 unlike bone, which has a rich blood supply. (3) Provides cushioning (shock absorption) between bones at joints. (4) Cannot repair itself easily because it has no blood supply. Found at: ends of bones at joints (articular cartilage), tip of nose, outer ear (pinna), between vertebrae (intervertebral discs), in the trachea (windpipe).",memory_hook:"CARTILAGE = Cushion + Flexible + No blood (Can't Fix). Press your nose tip \u2014 that flexible part is cartilage. Touch your ear \u2014 also cartilage. Bone is hard; cartilage cushions."},{id:"fc07",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Can muscles push? Explain how muscles produce movement.",back:"NO \u2014 muscles CANNOT push. Muscles can only CONTRACT (shorten and thicken), which means they can only PULL. A contracting muscle pulls the bone it is attached to toward itself. To move a bone in TWO directions, you need TWO muscles (working in opposing pairs). When one muscle contracts and pulls the bone one way, the other muscle relaxes. To pull the bone the other way, the second muscle contracts and the first one relaxes.",memory_hook:"MUSCLES = PULLERS only. No pushing allowed. To open a door your muscles PULL \u2014 even when you 'push' open a door, your arm muscles are pulling your arm backward. Muscles never, ever push."},{id:"fc08",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Explain how the biceps and triceps work together to bend and straighten the elbow.",back:"Bending the elbow (flexion): BICEPS (front of upper arm) contracts and shortens \u2192 pulls forearm upward. TRICEPS (back of upper arm) relaxes and lengthens. Straightening the elbow (extension): TRICEPS (back of upper arm) contracts and shortens \u2192 pulls forearm downward. BICEPS relaxes and lengthens. These two muscles are antagonists \u2014 they always work in opposition. When one contracts, the other relaxes. This paired-muscle system is necessary because each muscle can only pull, never push.",memory_hook:"BI bends = BICEPS bends elbow. TRI tries to straighten = TRICEPS straightens elbow. BEfore you bend, Biceps is the star; To straighten, Triceps Takes over."},{id:"fc09",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the vertebral column? How many vertebrae does it have, and what does it protect?",back:"The vertebral column (also called the backbone or spine) is a column of 33 vertebrae (individual bones) stacked on top of each other with cartilage discs (intervertebral discs) between them. It: (1) Provides structural support for the body \u2014 allows us to stand upright. (2) Protects the spinal cord \u2014 the main nerve pathway connecting the brain to the rest of the body. (3) Allows bending and twisting \u2014 because it is made of many individual vertebrae joined flexibly.",memory_hook:"33 vertebrae like 33 coins stacked in a column. The column SUPPORTS and PROTECTS the spinal cord inside. Bend the column, bend your back."},{id:"fc10",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does an earthworm move? What role do setae play?",back:"An earthworm has no bones or legs. It moves using two sets of muscles: (1) Circular muscles \u2014 wrap around each segment; when they contract, the segment becomes thin and long. (2) Longitudinal muscles \u2014 run along the length; when they contract, the segment becomes short and fat. Alternating contraction of these muscles creates a wave-like motion through the body. Setae: tiny bristle-like structures on each body segment that anchor into the soil, preventing the worm from slipping backward as it moves forward.",memory_hook:"EARTHWORM = Circular (long) + Longitudinal (short) muscles alternating = wave forward. SETAE = grip studs (like cleats on football boots). Thin and long \u2192 short and fat \u2192 moves forward."},{id:"fc11",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does a snail move?",back:"A snail has no skeleton. It moves using a single large muscular foot (the flat, broad underside of its body). The muscular foot creates wave-like contractions that ripple from back to front, pushing the snail forward. The snail also produces mucus (slime) that: (1) Lubricates the surface, reducing friction. (2) Helps the foot grip the surface. (3) Allows the snail to move on surfaces even upside down (glass, leaves). The trail of slime left behind a snail is evidence of this lubrication.",memory_hook:"SNAIL = 1 foot + slime. Muscular foot ripples. Slime makes it smooth. Snail's one-foot gliding act \u2014 slow but steady."},{id:"fc12",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How is a cockroach's body adapted for movement? How many legs does it have?",back:"A cockroach has: (1) 3 pairs of legs = 6 legs total. The legs are jointed and enable fast running. (2) 2 pairs of wings \u2014 most cockroaches can fly short distances. (3) An exoskeleton \u2014 a hard outer shell made of chitin that protects the body (replaces the internal skeleton). The body is divided into: head, thorax (to which legs and wings are attached), and abdomen. The jointed legs allow rapid, agile movement in all directions.",memory_hook:"Cockroach = 6 legs (3 pairs) + 4 wings (2 pairs) + 1 exoskeleton. 6-4-1. Three pairs of legs = fast scurrying. No internal bones \u2014 exoskeleton outside."},{id:"fc13",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does a fish move through water? What is the function of its tail and fins?",back:"Fish have a streamlined body (tapers at both ends) that reduces resistance to water flow. Movement: Body muscles create side-to-side waves that move from head to tail \u2014 this undulation propels the fish forward. Tail fin (caudal fin): primary propulsion \u2014 its sweeping motion provides the main thrust to move forward. Fins and their functions: Pectoral fins (chest sides) \u2014 steering left/right, braking. Dorsal fin (back top) \u2014 stability, prevents rolling. Pelvic fins (belly) \u2014 stability. Swim bladder: a gas-filled sac inside that helps the fish control its buoyancy (depth in water).",memory_hook:"Fish: STREAMLINED body + TAIL for thrust + FINS for steering/stability. Like a car: engine (tail) + steering wheel (pectoral fins) + balance (dorsal fin)."},{id:"fc14",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What adaptations allow birds to fly? Name four features.",back:"(1) Hollow bones: bird bones are hollow with internal struts \u2014 much lighter than solid bones, reducing body weight for flight. (2) Wings: modified forelimbs covered with flight feathers. Wings create lift (upward force) and thrust (forward force). (3) Streamlined body: teardrop/spindle shape reduces air resistance. (4) Strong breast (pectoral) muscles: very powerful muscles that flap the wings. (5) Feathers: lightweight, provide insulation and aerodynamic surface. (6) Keen eyesight: for navigation and hunting while flying.",memory_hook:"BIRD flies because: Hollow bones (light), Wings (modified arms), Streamlined shape (aerodynamic), Breast muscles (powerful). HWSB = 'He Wins Soaring By' these four adaptations."},{id:"fc15",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does a snake move without legs? What role do its scales and vertebrae play?",back:"A snake has no limbs but moves effectively using: (1) Many vertebrae: snakes may have 200\u2013400 vertebrae (compared to 33 in humans). This gives the body extreme flexibility. (2) Body muscles: muscles attached to the many vertebrae create waves of muscle contractions that move from head to tail. (3) Ventral scales (belly scales/scutes): large, flat scales on the snake's underside that grip the ground surface. As the body waves move, these scales push against the ground, propelling the snake forward (like oars pushing against water). The combination of flexible spine + muscular waves + scale grip = efficient slithering locomotion.",memory_hook:"SNAKE = many vertebrae + belly scales + muscle waves. No legs needed when you have 400 vertebrae and grip scales. The scales are the snake's 'feet'."},{id:"fc16",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Which joint allows you to nod your head 'yes' and which allows you to shake your head 'no'?",back:"Nodding 'YES' (up and down): This is a HINGE-like movement at the joint between the skull and the first cervical vertebra (atlas). The skull rocks forward and backward on the atlas. Shaking 'NO' (left and right rotation): This is a PIVOT joint \u2014 the atlas (1st vertebra) rotates around the axis (2nd vertebra). This allows the entire head and atlas to rotate left and right together around the axis. Two different joints for two different head movements \u2014 both in the neck.",memory_hook:"YES = up-down = atlas-skull (hinge-like). NO = left-right = atlas rotates on axis (pivot). 'At last I can say YES; Atlas-Axis makes me say NO.'"},{id:"fc17",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why do birds have hollow bones? Would a hollow bone be weaker or stronger than a solid bone?",back:"Birds have hollow bones to reduce body weight \u2014 essential for flight, because generating enough lift to fly requires minimal body weight. However, hollow bones are NOT weaker: bird hollow bones have internal struts and supports (like a truss bridge) inside the hollow cavity. This engineering design maintains structural strength while drastically reducing weight. Some birds' bones are actually lighter than their feathers! A hollow bone with internal supports can be as strong as a solid bone of much greater weight.",memory_hook:"Hollow + internal struts = strong AND light. Like a hollow metal tube vs a solid bar \u2014 the tube is lighter but nearly as strong. Birds solved this millions of years ago."},{id:"fc18",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Yakshagana is a classical dance-drama from coastal Karnataka. Performers make sweeping arm circles and dramatic hip movements. Which joints make these movements possible?",back:"Sweeping arm circles (full rotation of the arm): ball and socket joint at the SHOULDER \u2014 this joint allows movement in all directions including full 360\xb0 rotation. Dramatic hip movements (swaying, rotation): ball and socket joint at the HIP \u2014 same type of joint allows forward, backward, sideways, and rotational hip movement. Yakshagana's dramatic poses also use: hinge joints (elbow and knee for bending), pivot joint (neck for head rotation), and gliding joints (wrist for hand gestures). Ball and socket joints at shoulder and hip are the stars of Yakshagana movement.",memory_hook:"Yakshagana arm circle = shoulder ball-and-socket. Hip sway = hip ball-and-socket. Both = full-range movement from ball-in-socket design."},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the role of tendons? How are they different from cartilage?",back:"Tendons: strong, cord-like fibrous tissue that connects MUSCLES to BONES. When a muscle contracts, the tendon transmits the pulling force to the bone, causing movement. Tendons are not flexible \u2014 they are strong and inextensible (do not stretch much). Cartilage: smooth, cushioning tissue found at the ends of bones at JOINTS. Cartilage is flexible and smooth, reducing friction between bones. Key difference: Tendon = muscle-to-bone connector (transmits force). Cartilage = joint cushion between bones (reduces friction). Neither is a muscle or a bone \u2014 they are supporting connective tissues.",memory_hook:"TENDON = Ties muscle to bone (T-T-B). CARTILAGE = Cushions at joints (C-C). Tendon transmits force; cartilage cushions the impact."},{id:"fc20",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Compare how an earthworm and a snake move. What is similar and what is different?",back:"Similarities: Both use wave-like muscular contractions to move. Neither has limbs (legs or arms). Both use structures on their body to grip the surface. Differences: Earthworm has circular AND longitudinal muscles alternating (changes body shape dramatically). Snake uses muscles along the vertebral column \u2014 does not change shape as dramatically. Earthworm uses setae (tiny bristles) to grip soil. Snake uses ventral scales (large belly scales) to grip surfaces. Snake has a bony vertebral column (many vertebrae). Earthworm has no bones at all \u2014 its movement is entirely muscular.",memory_hook:"Both wave and grip \u2014 but earthworm is all-muscle, snake has a spine. Earthworm = muscle accordion. Snake = bony whip with scale-grips."}]}},2903,[]);